---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
export function getStaticPaths(){
const studies = {
  'workforce-infrastructure': {
    number:'01',category:'PRODUCTION BACKEND',title:'Workforce infrastructure',
    intro:'Modernising the backend behind workforce scheduling, compliance and payments.',
    context:'A workforce platform needs to coordinate worker assignments, declarations, wages and invoices while preserving business rules across time-sensitive changes. Legacy serverless workflows made separation of responsibilities, retries and consistency harder to reason about.',
    role:'Worked as the sole backend engineer on a major modernization effort, designing modular FastAPI services, an expanded PostgreSQL domain schema and asynchronous business workflows.',
    outcome:'Migrated more than 50 Cloud Functions to FastAPI; designed a 46-table PostgreSQL schema. The FastAPI service layer went into production with the existing Firestore database; the proposed PostgreSQL data migration was not completed during this phase.',
    challenges:[
      {name:'Separate business rules',detail:'Reworked function-based paths into service-oriented FastAPI routes, making validations, dependencies and execution boundaries clearer.'},
      {name:'Design the data model',detail:'Modelled shifts, assignments, payments and linked entities in a 46-table PostgreSQL schema with transaction and access-control considerations. This was design work, not a claim of a fully completed production data migration.'},
      {name:'Build for failure',detail:'Worked on background processing for external compliance requests, retries, queue separation and reconciliation so slow or failed integrations could be tracked.'}
    ],
    lessons:'A migration is successful only when the new architecture preserves the real business rules. Shipping a useful backend layer and deferring an unsafe database cutover can be the right engineering decision.',
    stack:['Python','FastAPI','PostgreSQL','Firestore','Redis','Celery']
  },
  antagents:{
    number:'02',category:'AI WORKFLOW ENGINEERING',title:'AntAgents',
    intro:'An automation platform that makes AI steps part of reliable business workflows.',
    context:'A useful agent platform cannot stop at calling a language model. It needs a workflow definition, tenant boundaries, execution state and failure visibility so users can understand and trust the result.',
    role:'Contributed across NestJS APIs, a React Flow visual builder, AI integrations and workflow orchestration alongside engineering teammates.',
    outcome:'Worked on agent-enabled workflow delivery recognised in the GeekyAnts appraisal, including execution flow, pause/resume and observability concerns. The wider system spanned multiple distributed services.',
    challenges:[
      {name:'Make workflows visible',detail:'Used React Flow concepts to represent automation as connected steps rather than hiding execution inside one API call.'},
      {name:'Coordinate long-running work',detail:'Worked with Temporal workflow patterns and asynchronous processing to represent state, retries and resumable execution.'},
      {name:'Observe failures',detail:'Integrated distributed-system tooling and execution tracing patterns so teams could inspect runs, errors and service behaviour.'}
    ],
    lessons:'Agent orchestration is largely about predictable software engineering: explicit state, well-defined inputs, retries and enough observability to understand a failed run.',
    stack:['TypeScript','NestJS','React Flow','Temporal','Redis','Docker']
  },
  'dealroom-ai':{
    number:'03',category:'HACKATHON / TEAM SECOND PLACE',title:'Dealroom.ai',
    intro:'A collaborative hackathon project exploring AI-powered proposal creation.',
    context:'Preparing a technical proposal from a client request involves reading large documents, extracting needs, identifying ambiguity, suggesting an architecture and turning that information into something the client can explore.',
    role:'Team hackathon contribution. The repo documents the architecture and early prototype; the precise division of work across team members has not yet been documented publicly.',
    outcome:'Our team placed second. The repository checklist records a working document-upload and parsing flow with streamed progress; the multi-agent war room, strategies and interactive proposal remained planned at the point recorded.',
    challenges:[
      {name:'Read real inputs',detail:'Built a FastAPI intake path for PDF, DOCX and spreadsheet files, with a Next.js upload interface and parsing feedback streamed to the browser.'},
      {name:'Plan the agent pipeline',detail:'Designed an analyst–architect–estimator workflow with explicit state, interactive feedback and structured decisions rather than one unbounded prompt.'},
      {name:'Scope an ambitious idea',detail:'Separated working demo capabilities from the longer-term proposal and analytics vision, making it clear what was actually implemented.'}
    ],
    lessons:'Hackathons are not just about finishing every planned feature. A good team can define an ambitious product vision, deliver a convincing core and explain what should be built next.',
    stack:['Next.js','TypeScript','FastAPI','Python','SSE','SQLite'],
    repository:'https://github.com/VishvendraTomar/dealroom.ai',
    repositoryNote:'Repository is currently private; the link may require access.'
  },
  'ai-developer-tooling':{
    number:'04',category:'APPLIED AI',title:'AI developer tooling',
    intro:'Using language models and retrieval to make repository understanding and code review more useful.',
    context:'Engineering teams work with large codebases, scattered context and repetitive review tasks. Model outputs can help, but they need relevant source context, structure and validation to be useful.',
    role:'Worked on AI-assisted code analysis and development tooling, including semantic search and LLM-assisted review workflows.',
    outcome:'Contributed repository analysis and engineering tooling, and independently researched techniques for improving AI-generated coding rules adopted in team practices.',
    challenges:[
      {name:'Find the right context',detail:'Used embeddings and retrieval concepts to identify relevant project information before generating explanations.'},
      {name:'Work with model output',detail:'Explored structured responses and practical handling of unreliable or incomplete model-generated suggestions.'},
      {name:'Improve team practices',detail:'Researched AI-assisted IDE techniques and translated those findings into better coding guidelines and rules for the team.'}
    ],
    lessons:'AI tooling becomes valuable when it fits existing development workflows and gives engineers useful, reviewable results rather than unverified answers.',
    stack:['Python','LLM APIs','Embeddings','Retrieval','Backend APIs']
  }
};
return Object.entries(studies).map(([slug,study])=>({params:{slug},props:{study}}));
}
const {study}=Astro.props;
---
<Layout title={study.title+' — Vishvendra Tomar'} description={study.intro}>
<main class="case">
  <header class="top wrap"><a href="/" class="back">← BACK TO PORTFOLIO</a><span>VT / CASE STUDY {study.number}</span></header>
  <section class="intro wrap"><div class="kicker"><span class="square"></span>{study.category} / {study.number}</div><h1>{study.title}<span>.</span></h1><p>{study.intro}</p><a href="#breakdown" class="skiplink">EXPLORE THE BREAKDOWN ↓</a></section>
  <div class="blue-stripe"></div>
  <section class="paper" id="breakdown"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="two-col"><div class="section-heading"><span class="index">01 /</span><h2>The context.</h2></div><div><p>{study.context}</p><div class="role"><span>MY FOCUS</span><p>{study.role}</p></div><div class="role outcome"><span>WHAT WAS DELIVERED</span><p>{study.outcome}</p></div>{study.repository && <div class="repo-link"><a href={study.repository} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VIEW GITHUB REPOSITORY ↗</a><small>{study.repositoryNote}</small></div>}</div></div>
    <div class="section-heading engineering"><span class="index">02 /</span><h2>Engineering decisions.</h2></div>
    <div class="decisions">{study.challenges.map((item,i)=><article class="decision"><span class="step">0{i+1}</span><h3>{item.name}</h3><p>{item.detail}</p></article>)}</div>
    <div class="two-col lesson"><div class="section-heading"><span class="index">03 /</span><h2>What matters.</h2></div><div><p>{study.lessons}</p><div class="stack">{study.stack.map(t=><span>{t}</span>)}</div></div></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="closing wrap"><div><span class="kicker">NEXT STEP</span><h2>Keep exploring<span>.</span></h2></div><a href="/#work">ALL SELECTED WORK ↗</a></section>
  <footer class="footer wrap"><span>© {new Date().getFullYear()} VISHVENDRA TOMAR</span><a href="/">BACK TO HOME ↗</a></footer>
</main>
</Layout>
<style>
:global(:root){--charcoal:#1e1e1c;--paper:#d8cbb3;--blue:#2f6bff}
.case{background:var(--charcoal);color:var(--paper);overflow:hidden}
.wrap{width:min(1120px,calc(100% - 80px));margin:auto}
.top{height:94px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:18px;border-bottom:1px solid #625d54;font:10px 'DM Mono',monospace;letter-spacing:1px}
.top span{color:#aea28f}.back{transition:color .2s}.back:hover{color:#89aaff}
.intro{padding:105px 0 110px}.kicker{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:13px;font:11px 'DM Mono',monospace;letter-spacing:1px;color:#b2a791}.square{display:inline-block;width:10px;height:10px;background:var(--blue)}
.intro h1{max-width:950px;font:500 clamp(62px,9vw,120px)/1 'Space Grotesk',sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.078em;margin:31px 0 37px}.intro h1 span,.closing h2 span{color:var(--blue)}
.intro p{max-width:660px;font:20px/1.7 'DM Sans',sans-serif;color:#b9ad9d;margin:0}.skiplink{display:inline-block;margin-top:55px;border-bottom:1px solid #b0a695;padding-bottom:10px;font:11px 'DM Mono',monospace;letter-spacing:1px}
.blue-stripe{height:7px;background:var(--blue)}.paper{background:var(--paper);color:var(--charcoal);padding:110px 0 125px}.two-col{display:grid;grid-template-columns:42% 1fr;gap:60px;align-items:start}
.section-heading .index{font:11px 'DM Mono',monospace;color:var(--blue)}h2{font:500 clamp(38px,5vw,68px)/1.08 'Space Grotesk',sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.06em;margin:22px 0 0}.two-col>div>p{font:19px/1.8 'DM Sans',sans-serif;margin:0;color:#514d46}.role{border-top:1px solid #b0a694;padding-top:20px;margin-top:42px}.role span{font:10px 'DM Mono',monospace;color:#666052}.role p{font:15px/1.75 'DM Sans',sans-serif;margin:12px 0}
.engineering{margin-top:120px;margin-bottom:40px}.decisions{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:20px}.decision{border-top:1px solid #a99e8d;padding:20px 12px 20px 0;min-height:220px}.step{font:11px 'DM Mono',monospace;color:var(--blue)}.decision h3{font:500 27px/1.2 'Space Grotesk',sans-serif;letter-spacing:-1px;margin:30px 0 12px}.decision p{font:14px/1.8 'DM Sans',sans-serif;color:#625c52}
.lesson{border-top:1px solid #aaa08d;padding-top:55px;margin-top:90px}.stack{display:flex;gap:9px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:28px}.stack span{border:1px solid #a99e8d;padding:9px 11px;font:10px 'DM Mono',monospace}
.closing{padding-top:95px;padding-bottom:95px;display:flex;align-items:end;justify-content:space-between;gap:30px}.closing h2{margin-top:21px}.closing>a{font:12px 'DM Mono',monospace;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--paper);white-space:nowrap}.footer{padding:27px 0;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;border-top:1px solid #625d54;font:10px 'DM Mono',monospace;color:#a99d8b}
@media(max-width:680px){.wrap{width:calc(100% - 38px)}.top{height:75px;font-size:9px}.intro{padding:78px 0 85px}.intro h1{font-size:clamp(50px,11vw,75px)}.intro p{font-size:16px}.skiplink{margin-top:40px}.paper{padding:70px 0 85px}.two-col{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:25px}.two-col>div>p{font-size:17px}.engineering{margin-top:75px}.decisions{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:3px}.decision{min-height:0;padding:23px 0}.decision h3{margin:15px 0}.lesson{margin-top:65px}.closing{padding-top:70px;padding-bottom:70px;display:block}.closing>a{display:inline-block;margin-top:25px}.footer{font-size:9px}}

/* Long project names and header links should never collide on a small phone. */
.intro h1,.section-heading h2,.decision h3,.closing h2{overflow-wrap:break-word}
@media(max-width:480px){
  .intro h1{font-size:clamp(39px,10.2vw,53px);letter-spacing:-.065em;line-height:1.06}
  .top{gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;min-height:75px;height:auto;padding-top:16px;padding-bottom:16px;row-gap:8px}
  .top span{font-size:8px}
  .intro{padding:68px 0 74px}
  .kicker{font-size:9px;letter-spacing:.5px}
  .section-heading h2{font-size:clamp(36px,10vw,50px)}
  .two-col>div>p{font-size:16px}
  .closing h2{font-size:clamp(40px,11vw,58px)}
  .footer{flex-wrap:wrap;row-gap:9px}
}
.outcome{margin-top:25px}.outcome p{font-weight:500;color:#38342e}.repo-link{margin-top:27px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:flex-start;gap:10px}.repo-link a{display:inline-block;padding-bottom:8px;border-bottom:1px solid #3c3932;font:11px 'DM Mono',monospace}.repo-link a:hover{color:#2f6bff;border-color:#2f6bff}.repo-link small{color:#5d574e;font:11px/1.6 'DM Sans',sans-serif}</style>